triage: 10 services, severity warning, start from rate, 3 unmeasured edge(s) crossed

states: triaging -> planning -> investigating -> synthesizing -> proposing
trajectory: 71bdd76d-2d69-4259-aaa9-256c20c94e32
triage judged: investigate (medium confidence, suspects resource_exhaustion)
  Six MongoDB instances alerting simultaneously plus two dependent services points to a shared datastore-layer failure, not a single noisy latency warning. Simultaneity across independent DB instances suggests a shared host/resource constraint worth investigating.
investigation: 2 round(s), 7 dispatch(es), 22 step(s)

=== PROPOSAL ===
  ABSTAINED - no permitted action fits the evidence
  if wrong    : This abstention is wrong if the corrected mongodb-rate log query comes back empty-but-successful with the container reported Running and no change records in the preceding 24h — that would meet restart_service's precondition properly and make a restart of mongodb-rate the right call, with rate's crash-loop ceasing and serving log lines appearing within about 120s. It is also wrong if the delay itself is costly: rate and reservation are already down and frontend and search are degraded, so every minute spent on diagnostics is user-visible outage. If the operator judges that cost higher than the cost of an irreversible restart on an unverified target, they should override this and restart mongodb-rate.
  rests on    : tr_88a01606ee27, tr_46428f62c4a4, tr_1c9653913a48, tr_f6ed97dbcbce
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 21613 pushed / 2310 pulled (pull rate 0.0966), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : process_freeze
  fix class   : restart
  confidence  : low
  evidence    : tr_88a01606ee27, tr_46428f62c4a4, tr_bfcbc09c5459, tr_f6ed97dbcbce, tr_4d406f3b84ac
  root cause  : rate never reaches serving: each pod reads config, logs the correct datastore URL mongodb-rate:27017, then panics ~12s later in database initialization with an unreachable-servers error, looping continuously [tr_88a01606ee27]. The dial deadline expiring at connect time, with no query-timeout or pool entries, points at mongodb-rate being present-but-unserving rather than at anything wrong inside rate. Config correctness, request-path bugs, and pool exhaustion in rate are each excluded by rate's own log [tr_88a01606ee27]. mongodb-rate's log query was rejected by Loki with a 400 on a malformed pod regex, so the culprit's own voice was never heard [tr_46428f62c4a4]; both metric baselines failed for structural instrumentation reasons [tr_bfcbc09c5459, tr_f6ed97dbcbce] and all change-history queries died on a parse error [tr_4d406f3b84ac, tr_1c9653913a48, tr_c183cd3a076b]. The surviving mechanism consistent with the one good signal is that mongodb-rate's process is stopped while its socket still exists, so rate hangs to its connect deadline and dies.
  OPEN        : mongodb-rate's own logs were never read (Loki 400) — re-run with a corrected pod selector to see whether mongod is silent (freeze), complaining it cannot reach anything (partition), refusing with no space left (disk fill), or absent entirely.
  OPEN        : Pod/container state for mongodb-rate: is it Running, CrashLoopBackOff, Pending, or evicted? No infrastructure-level check was performed.
  OPEN        : All change-history queries failed on a parse error, so a deploy, image, or config change to mongodb-rate or rate in the preceding 24h remains entirely unobserved; the window queried also started at onset rather than before it.
  OPEN        : Whether the other alerting mongodb-* services share the same failure, which would point to a node- or cluster-level cause rather than one datastore.

